186045417 true] [17592186054992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186054992 1071 "![](images/ae/aee38b4fa46a403ee9517fc5a087434c8205b6553a4ebefa43be74ba7babfbc0.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x, y)$ of the following system of equations:\n${y = x + 1; y = x^{2} + 1$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186054992 1071 "![](images/ae/aee38b4fa46a403ee9517fc5a087434c8205b6553a4ebefa43be74ba7babfbc0.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x+1 \\\\ y={x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186054992 1074 "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/56/563850386ee40e291a04911a2216614bda567ffbb8cfce1a7713400900d2701c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect at \n\n$\\,$ $(0,1)\\,$ and $\\,(1,2).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186055514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186055514 1071 "Simplify $\\textrm{i}^{50}$." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186055514 1071 "Simplify ${\\text{i}}^{50}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186055514 1074 "To find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=50.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get:\n\n$$\n50÷4=12\\,\\text{R}2\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{50}={\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059511 1071 "Which angles are coterminal with $240^\\circ$?\n\n$600^\\circ, \\quad 300^\\circ, \\quad -480^\\circ$." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186059511 1071 "Which angles are coterminal with $240^\\circ?$\n\n$$\n600^\\circ, \\quad 300^\\circ, \\quad -480^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059511 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta$, we add and subtract integer multiples of $360^\\circ\\mathbin{.}$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $360^\\circ$ to $240^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n240^{∘} + 360^{∘} = 600^{∘}✓\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $600^\\circ$, so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $360^\\circ$ from $240^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n240^{∘} - 360^{∘} &= -120^{∘} \\\\\n240^{∘} - 2 \\cdot (360^{∘}) &= -480^{∘}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-480^\\circ$, so we can stop here.\n\nSo, of the given angles, the coterminal angles are $600^\\circ$ and $-480^\\circ$." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186059511 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta,$ we add and subtract integer multiples of $360^\\circ\\mathbin{.}$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $360^\\circ$ to $240^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{240}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={600}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $600^\\circ,$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $360^\\circ$ from $240^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & =-{120}^{∘} \\\\ {240}^{∘}-2\\cdot ({360}^{∘}) & =-{480}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-480^\\circ,$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo, of the given angles, the coterminal angles are $600^\\circ$ and $-480^\\circ.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059512 1071 "Which angle is coterminal with $\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}$?\n\n$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{5}, \\quad -\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}, \\quad -\\dfrac{11\\pi}{5}$." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186059512 1071 "Which angle is coterminal with $\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}?$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\pi}{5}, \\quad -\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}, \\quad -\\dfrac{11\\pi}{5}.\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059512 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta$ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi$.\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi$ to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3π}{5} + 2π = \\frac{13π}{5}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {13 \\pi} {5}\n$$\n\nso we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi$ from\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3π}{5} - 2π &= -\\frac{7π}{5}✓ \\\\\n\\frac{3π}{5} - 2 \\cdot (2π) &= -\\frac{17π}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac {17 \\pi} {5}\n$$\n\nso we can stop here.\n\nSo, of the given angles, the only coterminal angle is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186059512 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta$ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi.$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi$ to $\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =\\frac{13\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $\\dfrac {13 \\pi} {5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi$ from $\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {5}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\pi }{5}-2\\pi  & =-\\frac{7\\pi }{5}\\,✓ \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi }{5}-2\\cdot (2\\pi ) & =-\\frac{17\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\dfrac {17 \\pi} {5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo, of the given angles, the only coterminal angle is $-\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059513 1071 "The angle $\\theta =  -435^\\circ$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059513 1071 "The angle $\\theta = -435^\\circ$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186059513 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-435^\\circ$ by *adding* integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-435^{∘} + 360^{∘} &= -75^{∘} \\\\\n- 75^{∘} + 360^{∘} &= 285^{∘}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the angle $285^\\circ$ is coterminal with $-435^\\circ$. Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/4f/4f3fc79c2fc99478c407a63352d9113ef9cbef6a93a6feb62bd284d5f443bed5.png)\n\nWe see that $285^\\circ$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-435^\\circ$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186059513 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-435^\\circ$ by *adding* integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{435}^{∘}+{360}^{∘} & =-{75}^{∘} \\\\ -{75}^{∘}+{360}^{∘} & ={285}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the angle $285^\\circ$ is coterminal with $-435^\\circ.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/4f/4f3fc79c2fc99478c407a63352d9113ef9cbef6a93a6feb62bd284d5f443bed5.png)\n\nWe see that $285^\\circ$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-435^\\circ$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059514 1071 "The angle $\\theta =  \\dfrac {8\\pi}3$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059514 1071 "The angle $\\theta = \\dfrac {8\\pi}3$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186059514 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {8\\pi}3\n$$\n\nby *subtracting* integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0, 2\\pi)$.\n\n$$\n\\frac{8π}{3} - 2π = \\frac{2π}{3}✓\n$$\n\nSo the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {2\\pi}3\n$$\n\nis coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {8\\pi}3\n$$\n\nLet's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/23/2360ed202deeca403c4631c319b69df30a9f0268b7ef270a8e436103c0ac3edb.png)\n\nWe see that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nlies in quadrant II. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nalso lies in this quadrant." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186059514 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac {8\\pi}3$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0, 2\\pi).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the angle $\\dfrac {2\\pi}3$ is coterminal with $\\dfrac {8\\pi}3.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/23/2360ed202deeca403c4631c319b69df30a9f0268b7ef270a8e436103c0ac3edb.png)\n\nWe see that $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $\\dfrac{8\\pi}{3}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537945 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537948 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537952 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537956 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059685 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537888 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537907 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537927 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186059936 1050 540.0 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186060901 1064 "All the angles we have constructed so far have been inside the range ![](images/fa/fa8919b9a8968214967b7c4b868b1eb64a95c1d0d2068bd312a4a4bb11ad4e6f.svg) However, we can also construct angles outside this range.\n\nThe way we do this involves **coterminal angles**. Coterminal angles are different angles that appear as the same angle in the coordinate plane. For example, the angles ![](images/d2/d22511f2212b05e9408f52f8da19a125897737f033bb64f480780f2f024b2091.svg) and ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) are coterminal because a rotation of ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) is the same as the rotation of ![](images/d5/d5c42dddda6dd2e3707f543621c5ab3ef3557e26aa8e938ffddc686917238fdd.svg) In general, whenever we add or subtract a multiple of ![](images/43/431e60581c842f0bf0e2554ccfbe65001d79fd5493de301c6889f8237304b725.svg) we get a coterminal angle.\n\nThe standard way in which we draw a **positive** angle ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) in the coordinate plane is as follows:\n\n- Subtract integer multiples of ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range ![](images/29/29577be589a8a6614c131d995797fa1224c1df2f6b538a669e5fba718edb926b.svg)\n\n- Draw the resulting angle in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, to represent the angle ![](images/fa/fae5f1a011f55464bbc26a61e49aa6c4b2e2b9fb2076370a05f35c00de3a7a29.svg) in the coordinate plane, we first subtract ![](images/29/2903c04e0275e76d718eff7ae8277ecb8b5404777225212dca2507b93907ab41.svg)\n\n![](images/3b/3b3bf36f7e1ccf241036659c484ffa0dd17884f8044f6bd6faf6240764552c41.svg)\n\nThe result, ![](images/73/73c6bd81124f4f9c92e0d8ed37449d8dd5be227f9e4b05b2f7953d28ddff7d6c.svg) is a coterminal angle that lies in the range ![](images/29/29577be589a8a6614c131d995797fa1224c1df2f6b538a669e5fba718edb926b.svg) So, to draw the angle ![](images/63/637324a8ad2f6d52227a2fe46d938a816aab3de3d50e0f9ee035f602537e2091.svg) we draw the coterminal angle ![](images/33/33cc1d85e29c1ae4ce3b019b0e49a5dba77efefa6f405590a91fce42f504baf3.svg) in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/bb/bbeb3dfdcb56e4f7f1a39010dc798af004f3ab5ee843f653e86f80fe9974dd30.png)\n\n**Note:** For every multiple of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) we subtract, we make one complete rotation counter-clockwise around the origin. A rotation of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) is the same as the rotation of ![](images/57/5792ebfa34e0824d805857dcb2467c1c23d3786ae5a5bad16b0c1f6616b6b0bf.svg) so it doesn't contribute to our final angle.\n\nHere, we subtracted ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) a single time, so we make a single rotation.\n\n![](images/ac/ac16cf651b6f5e16d3f31418646388dac16384c4824e0095bf9a925c13b1ea77.png)" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186060901 1064 "All the angles we have constructed so far have been inside the range $(-360^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$. However, we can also construct angles outside this range.\n\nThe way we do this involves **coterminal angles**. Coterminal angles are different angles that appear as the same angle in the coordinate plane. For example, the angles $0^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ$ are coterminal because a rotation of $360^\\circ$ is the same as the rotation of $0^\\circ$. In general, whenever we add or subtract a multiple of $360^\\circ$, we get a coterminal angle.\n\nThe standard way in which we draw a **positive** angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane is as follows:\n\n- Subtract integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$.\n- Draw the resulting angle in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, to represent the angle $405^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane, we first subtract $360^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n405^{∘} - 360^{∘} = 45^{∘}✓\n$$\n\nThe result, $45^\\circ$, is a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$. So, to draw the angle $405^\\circ$, we draw the coterminal angle $45^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/6c/6c96b1f8b34764c0d1a8ffb2362349d3ac3f4f99eaaf5c8e4c249fab1f29ceb3.png)\n\n**Note:** For every multiple of $\\color{blue}360^\\circ$ we subtract, we make one complete rotation counter-clockwise around the origin. A rotation of ${\\color{blue}360^\\circ}$ is the same as the rotation of ${\\color{blue}0^\\circ}$, so it doesn't contribute to our final angle.\n\nHere, we subtracted ${\\color{blue}360^\\circ}$ a single time, so we make a single rotation.\n\n![](images/dd/dd44ea334cc1cf709685a71a5a88025955e5cc1fb96cd9d938272b8024b99acc.png)" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186060902 1064 "To find a coterminal angle of a given angle ![](images/1f/1f216b45120e801bbdcf2c3f165ac341a7dd0bd305f87381647b205831b06725.svg) we rotate ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) clockwise or counter-clockwise from ![](images/b9/b98533dee87a7bd8ed4d593a2aabd517621d6ab96e40a5b2ab4a8ac1923ddac5.svg) We could do this again and again, each time looping by ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) to find an infinite number of coterminal angles:\n![](images/bc/bcd7ec5620afdf6f142437b0a0aedcddcee4678309d9d2328da3892eb0034730.svg)\n\nFor instance, the angles ![](images/ed/ed05bb3b845471f7526114d38f3c1a525aeb653d50a50b3ebe0457266e275d5f.svg) ![](images/94/9476642d5d3f3e418c71f7b062f33f894fa648e3c0cee6a15fc359b43889c19a.svg) ![](images/94/94f4422d3745b4401e7c9517c9e08382bb4d4af59a4f9ef164500530b530a525.svg) and ![](images/98/98e07f13eb10e759924519f7b068aa9f9768dcd7c362088b89f1831c886a3a2d.svg) are all coterminal.\n\n  \n\n![](images/c2/c2949532fb08722c3dbaaad3cd971ae2c3d3e7d7606f051da6dccd2bc3cfecc6.png)" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186060902 1064 "To find a coterminal angle of a given angle $\\theta$, we rotate $360^\\circ$ clockwise or counter-clockwise from $\\theta$. We could do this again and again, each time looping by $360^\\circ$ to find an infinite number of coterminal angles:\n$\\theta \\pm 360^\\circ n,\\quad \\text{where} n \\text{is any integer.}$\n\nFor instance, the angles $-240^\\circ$, $120^\\circ$, $480^\\circ$, and $840^\\circ$ are all coterminal.\n\n![](images/16/1684bdb452645ca612d0e0b08321b688f8d3e4a7a6de005a3afd620707eae9ee.png)" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186060903 1064 "The method for constructing a *negative* angle ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) outside the range ![](images/3b/3bbae843070f7afe0fc1c253cc8dd663ed4571cc5d250bebae728dd3298cee8a.svg) is similar to the method for positive angles, but with one main difference: we *add* multiples of ![](images/43/431e60581c842f0bf0e2554ccfbe65001d79fd5493de301c6889f8237304b725.svg) instead of subtracting. So the steps are:\n\n- Add integer multiples of ![](images/6f/6f3e3ccd469bd4b7cf2baf7a17cad64bb11f3f6f2d202e79362b8c44df1fcdd2.svg) until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range ![](images/29/29577be589a8a6614c131d995797fa1224c1df2f6b538a669e5fba718edb926b.svg)\n\n- Draw the resulting angle in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, we can represent the angle ![](images/78/78826f360e99c31e2e800e0691e6770af9a57442c1b09212bb94f3df8a7a7e6c.svg) as follows:\n\n![](images/bc/bcc5c8dedaeca80ac8c48f4d5d18aa3666e87b5be942f1a49d187f99ade28435.svg)\n\nNow, we draw the angle ![](images/20/20615d4147fe63007acc775caaa7d175edca7e0717a9e1224b4a66d490df2316.svg) in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/78/785973780f0ed538c0a0f430a402c25fb562a7308911f1d5cecc958da02b99f2.png)\n\nAgain, for every multiple of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) that we add, we make one complete rotation around the origin clockwise. A rotation of ![](images/4b/4b6a99f552f4ddb574063b55a576d5ba9d4a8fbc2be8b87c8eb11dfee8331c34.svg) is the same as the rotation of ![](images/57/5792ebfa34e0824d805857dcb2467c1c23d3786ae5a5bad16b0c1f6616b6b0bf.svg) so it doesn't contribute to our final angle." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186060903 1064 "The method for constructing a *negative* angle $\\theta$ outside the range $(-360^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$ is similar to the method for positive angles, but with one main difference: we *add* multiples of $360^\\circ$, instead of subtracting. So the steps are:\n\n- Add integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$.\n- Draw the resulting angle in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, we can represent the angle $-660^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-660^{∘} + 360^{∘} &= -300^{∘} \\\\\n- 300^{∘} + 360^{∘} &= 60^{∘}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the angle $60^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/e7/e76aff5a8e7bc805be56a61db114f111a9ab15f54b01eb3dd5542c1023224b85.png)\n\nAgain, for every multiple of $360^\\circ$ that we add, we make one complete rotation around the origin clockwise. A rotation of ${\\color{blue}360^\\circ}$ is the same as the rotation of ${\\color{blue}0^\\circ}$, so it doesn't contribute to our final angle." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186064220 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537957 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186068880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537894 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186068880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537938 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186068881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537950 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186068881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537989 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186068882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537984 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186068883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537988 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186068883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537992 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186068969 1050 780.0 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186086154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086155 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186086154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086156 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186086154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086157 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186086154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086158 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186086154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186086159 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186086154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537286 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186086154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537320 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186086154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537563 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186086154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537571 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186086154 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537686 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186086154 1078 #edb/ref 17592186537686 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087431 1071 "Given that $f(x) = x^2$, what is $f'(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186087431 1071 "Given that $f(x) = x^2,$ what is $f'(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087431 1074 "The derivative $f '(x)$ is given by the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2\n$$\n\n, we have that\n\n$$\nf(x+h) = (x+h)^2\n$$\n\nHence, we get\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f'(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186087431 1074 "The derivative $f '(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} .\n$$\n\nSince $f(x) = x^2$,  we have that $f(x+h) = (x+h)^2.$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f'(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0} \\dfrac{(x+h)^2-x^2}{h} .\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087432 1071 "Given $y =x^2$, calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ using the limit of a difference quotient." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186087432 1071 "Given $y =x^2,$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ using the limit of a difference quotient." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087432 1074 "We let $y=f(x)$ and apply the formula for the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= lim_(h → 0)(f(x + h) - f(x))/(h) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)((x + h)^{2} - x^{2})/(h) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)\\frac{x^{2} + 2xh + h^{2} - x^{2}}{h} \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)\\frac{2xh + h^{2}}{h} \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\frac{2xh}{h} + \\frac{h^{2}}{h}) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(2x + h) \\\\\n&= 2x + 0 \\\\\n&= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186087432 1074 "We let $y=f(x)$ and apply the formula for the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(x+h)^{2}-{x}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xh+{h}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2xh+{h}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2xh}{h}+\\frac{{h}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(2x+h) \\\\  & =2x+0 \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537924 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537935 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537937 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537897 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537959 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537884 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537953 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537931 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537947 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186087898 1050 420.0 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186088250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186088250 1071 "The equation $x^{2} - 8x + 52 = 0$ can be written in the form $(x + p)^{2} = q$. Find $p + q$, and solve the equation." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186088250 1071 "The equation ${x}^{2}-8x+52=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186088250 1074 "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+52 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x & =-52\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x & =-52 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =-52 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}-16 & =-52 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-4,\\,q=-36,$ and $p+q=-40.$\n\nFinally, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =-36 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-36} \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm 6\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =4\\pm 6\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=4+6\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=4-6\\,\\text{i}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186088899 1064 "The instantaneous rate of change of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$, denoted $f'(a)$, is given by the limit\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}\n$$\n\nThis formula works for a specific point $x=a$. If we want a method that works for a *general* point $x$, then we substitute $a=x$ in the formula and get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nThe function $f'(x)$ is called the **derivative of $f(x)$ with respect to $x$**. It gives the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at a general point with coordinates $(x,f(x))$." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186088899 1064 "The instantaneous rate of change of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a,$ denoted $f'(a),$ is given by the limit\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}.\n$$\n\nThis formula works for a specific point $x=a.$ If we want a method that works for a *general* point $x,$ then we substitute $a=x$ in the formula and get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f'(x)$ is called the **derivative of $f(x)$ with respect to $x$**. It gives the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at a general point with coordinates $(x,f(x)).$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186088900 1064 "For a given curve $y=f(x)$, the derivative can be represented using several different notations. They are\n\n$$\nf'(x),\\qquad y'(x), \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nIn words, the term $y'(x)$ is said like\n\n- \"$y$ prime of $x$,\" or\n- \"$y$ prime,\" or sometimes\n- \"$y$ dash.\"\n\nThe term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nis said like \"dee $y$ by dee $x$.\"\n\nThe important thing to remember is that they all represent the same thing, the derivative." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186088900 1064 "For a given curve $y=f(x),$ the derivative can be represented using several different notations. They are\n\n$$\nf'(x),\\qquad y'(x), \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nIn words, the term $y'(x)$  is said like\n\n- \"$y$ prime of $x$,\" or\n\n- \"$y$ prime,\" or sometimes\n\n- \"$y$ dash.\"\n\nThe term $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ is said like \"dee $y$ by dee $x$.\"\n\nThe important thing to remember is that they all represent the same thing, the derivative." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186098511 1078 #edb/ref 17592186098514 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186101018 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537977 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186101019 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537899 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186101019 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537911 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186101020 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537936 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186101196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186101196 1071 "A cinema hall can hold $120$ people. The price of each ticket is $$10$. The cost of maintaining the cinema is made up of a fixed cost of $$400$ plus a cost of $$0.25$ per person. How many tickets should the cinema sell to maximize its profit?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186101196 1071 "A cinema hall can hold $120$ people. The price of each ticket is $$10.$ The cost of maintaining the cinema is made up of a fixed cost of $$400$ plus a cost of $$0.25$ per person. How many tickets should the cinema sell to maximize its profit?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186101196 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ tickets, the cinema has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=10\\cdot x=10x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{400}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{0.25x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =10x-(400+0.25x) \\\\  & =9.75x-400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 120.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=120$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<120.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=9.75.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nOur candidates are $x=0$ and $x=120.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =9.75(0)-400=-400 \\\\ P(120) & =9.75(120)-400=770\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the cinema will have a maximum profit level when it sells $120$ tickets." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186107628 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537646 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186107628 1078 #edb/ref 17592186537646 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110289 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac12 e^{x+8}-2$." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186110289 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac12 e^{x+8}-2.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110289 1074 "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{2}e^{x + 8} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}e^{y + 8} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}e^{y + 8} - 2 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}e^{y + 8} &= x + 2 \\\\\ne^{y + 8} &= 2x + 4 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{y + 8}) &= \\ln (2x + 4) \\\\\ny + 8 &= \\ln (2x + 4) \\\\\ny &= \\ln (2x + 4) - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(2x+4)} - 8\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186110289 1074 "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x+8}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{y+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{y+8}-2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{y+8} & =x+2 \\\\ {e}^{y+8} & =2x+4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y+8 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(2x+4)} - 8.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110500 1071 "What is the inverse of $f(x) =\\ln(x+3)+6$?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186110500 1071 "What is the inverse of $f(x) =\\ln(x+3)+6?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110500 1074 "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x+3)+6\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\ln (y + 3) + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\ln (y + 3) + 6 \\\\\n\\ln (y + 3) &= x - 6 \\\\\ne^{\\ln (y + 3)} &= e^{x - 6} \\\\\ny + 3 &= e^{x - 6} \\\\\ny &= e^{x - 6} - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = e^{x-6}-3\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186110500 1074 "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\ln(x+3)+6\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3)+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3)+6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3) & =x-6 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+3)} & ={e}^{x-6} \\\\ y+3 & ={e}^{x-6} \\\\ y & ={e}^{x-6}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) = e^{x-6}-3.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110501 1071 "The function $f(x) = e^{x-k}+2$ has an inverse function given by $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(x-2)}+4$. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186110501 1071 "The function $f(x) = e^{x-k}+2$ has an inverse function given by $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(x-2)}+4.$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110501 1074 "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= e^{x - k} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= e^{y - k} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= e^{y - k} + 2 \\\\\ne^{y - k} &= x - 2 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{y - k}) &= \\ln (x - 2) \\\\\ny - k &= \\ln (x - 2) \\\\\ny &= \\ln (x - 2) + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(x-2)} + k\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=4$." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186110501 1074 "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{x-k}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y-k}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y-k}+2 \\\\ {e}^{y-k} & =x-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y-k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) \\\\ y-k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) = \\ln{(x-2)} + k.$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=4.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110502 1071 "What is the inverse of $f(x) = 2\\log_4(3x)-1$?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186110502 1071 "What is the inverse of $f(x) = 2\\log_4(3x)-1?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110502 1074 "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2\\log_{4} (3x) - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2\\log_{4} (3y) - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2\\log_{4} (3y) - 1 \\\\\n2\\log_{4} (3y) &= x + 1 \\\\\n\\log_{4} (3y) &= \\frac{x + 1}{2} \\\\\n4^{\\log_{4} (3y)} &= 4^{(x + 1)/2} \\\\\n3y &= 4^{(x + 1)/2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{3}4^{(x + 1)/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) =\\dfrac13 4^{(x+1)/2}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186110502 1074 "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3x)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3y)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{log}_{4}⁡(3y)-1 \\\\ 2{log}_{4}⁡(3y) & =x+1 \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(3y) & =\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(3y)} & ={4}^{(x+1)/2} \\\\ 3y & ={4}^{(x+1)/2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}{4}^{(x+1)/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is $f^{-1}(x) =\\dfrac13 4^{(x+1)/2}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537883 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537966 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110607 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537983 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110608 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537941 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110608 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537958 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110700 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537918 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110700 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537921 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110700 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537946 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110700 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537960 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110700 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537976 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537896 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537901 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537942 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537979 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537990 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537919 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537940 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537964 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537965 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537986 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537880 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537882 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537889 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537905 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110841 1050 720.0 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110985 1071 "The inverse of the function $f(x) = 5\\log_{4} (10x - 1)$ is $f^{-1}(x) =$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186110985 1071 "The inverse of the function $f(x)=5{log}_{4}⁡(10x-1)$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186110985 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5{log}_{4}⁡(10x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{log}_{4}⁡(10y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{log}_{4}⁡(10y-1) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(10y-1) & =\\frac{x}{5} \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡(10y-1)} & ={4}^{x/5} \\\\ 10y-1 & ={4}^{x/5} \\\\ 10y & ={4}^{x/5}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{4}^{x/5}+1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{4}^{x/5}+1}{10}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186111881 1064 "Now suppose we wish to find the inverse of the logarithmic function\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1\n$$\n\nWe can find $f^{-1}(x)$ using the same three steps as before.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx = 3\\log_{2}(y-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\log_{2} (y - 1) + 1 &= x \\\\\n3\\log_{2} (y - 1) &= x - 1 \\\\\n\\log_{2} (y - 1) &= \\frac{x - 1}{3} \\\\\n2^{\\log_{2} (y - 1)} &= 2^{(x - 1)/3} \\\\\ny - 1 &= 2^{(x - 1)/3} \\\\\ny &= 2^{(x - 1)/3} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = {2}^{(x-1)/3}+1\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186111881 1064 "Now suppose we wish to find the inverse of the logarithmic function \n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1.\n$$\n\nWe can find $f^{-1}(x)$ using the same three steps as before.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = 3\\log_{2}(x-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = 3\\log_{2}(y-1)+1\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{2}⁡(y-1)+1 & =x \\\\ 3{log}_{2}⁡(y-1) & =x-1 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(y-1) & =\\frac{x-1}{3} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(y-1)} & ={2}^{(x-1)/3} \\\\ y-1 & ={2}^{(x-1)/3} \\\\ y & ={2}^{(x-1)/3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = {2}^{(x-1)/3}+1.\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186111905 1064 "Suppose we have the transformed exponential function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2^{5x}+1\n$$\n\nand we wish to find the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$. We can do this using the following three steps.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2^{5x} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2^{5y} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2^{5y} + 1 \\\\\nx - 1 &= 2^{5y} \\\\\n\\log_{2} (x - 1) &= \\log_{2} (2^{5y}) \\\\\n\\log_{2} (x - 1) &= 5y \\\\\n5y &= \\log_{2} (x - 1) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{5}\\log_{2} (x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\log_2{(x-1)}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186111905 1064 "Suppose we have the transformed exponential function \n\n$$\nf(x)=2^{5x}+1\n$$\n\nand we wish to find the inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$ We can do this using the following three steps.\n\n**Step 1:** We start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{5x}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Then, we interchange $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5y}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5y}+1 \\\\ x-1 & ={2}^{5y} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x-1) & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{5y}) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x-1) & =5y \\\\ 5y & ={log}_{2}⁡(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{5}{log}_{2}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}\\log_2{(x-1)}.\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186130990 1078 #edb/ref 17592186130992 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186133788 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537904 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186133789 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537968 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186133882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537906 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186133930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537920 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186133930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537972 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186133931 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537908 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186133931 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537916 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186134068 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e63a57e50b60d7f0c6a3ce12d164716e2cba71c6bd4c6df10460a35beba4209.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 2x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186134068 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e63a57e50b60d7f0c6a3ce12d164716e2cba71c6bd4c6df10460a35beba4209.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186134139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186134139 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x =-1$, $x = 1$, $y = 0$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = 1$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F = 2xzi + (x^{2} + z)j + 2k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186134139 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward