\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186176223 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/de/deeb5648e328ecc93cf6c1de620e103da0f7677705d1e89e4654deff8d411f66.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=7x+\\sqrt{y+5},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dx+(7x+\\sqrt{y+5})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186176311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186176311 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨x{y}^{2},\\,x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186176311 1071 "Given that\n$∮_(C)F \\cdot dr = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}AAAAAAAAdrdθ$,\nwhere $F(x, y) = ⟨xy^{2}, x + e^{\\cos y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186176311 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+(x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d831c7fb805d16ec7a2dc2462813ea79b78d888ed2121330ed5d823b590d3c4f.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+(x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r-{r}^{3}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186179646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537933 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186179646 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537939 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186186972 1078 #edb/ref 17592186186975 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186188359 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537932 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186188360 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537974 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186188782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186188782 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have $Var[X] = 3$, $Var[Y] = 6$, and $Cov[X, Y] = 2$. Find $Var[4X + 2Y]$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186188782 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=3,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=6,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=2.$ Find $\\text{Var}[4X+2Y].$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186188782 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[4X+2Y] & ={4}^{2}\\cdot 3+{2}^{2}\\cdot 6+2\\cdot 4\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =48+24+32 \\\\  & =104.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186191829 1078 #edb/ref 17592186191832 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186192832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537969 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186192832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537978 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186192833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537922 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186192833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537951 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186193317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186193317 1071 "A scientist measured $5$ particular 