re arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.5)^{2}+(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =30.26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the scientist used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{29}{30.26} \\\\  & ≈0.958 \\\\  & =95.8%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186198390 1038 #edb/ref 17592186537913 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186230302 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230303 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186230302 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230304 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186230302 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230305 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186230302 1075 #edb/ref 17592186230306 13194139535892 17592186045417 false] [17592186230302 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537298 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186230302 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537346 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186230302 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537354 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186230302 1075 #edb/ref 17592186537506 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186230302 1078 #edb/ref 17592186537298 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186231572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186231572 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-2,2]$, where $f$ is $4$-periodic and odd. Which Fourie