0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6x-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{3}-\\frac{3(2)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =16-12 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186244233 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/5f/5f34ac74599937e0ae181126ff9a3da5b29dfe213614a1f23ca10c7062f22f7d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy-1$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6x-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(2x-{x}