ff656e20-f3df-5084-9cd4-4ad94db5cfe5" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537788 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537879 1069 "q-96324" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537879 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{1-{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537879 1072 #uuid "007da683-6a06-5103-8ccf-d7e5e491284b" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537879 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1-(1+{sinh}^{2}⁡x)}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537873 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537880 1069 "q-90426" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537880 1071 "The function $f(x)={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x-1}{k})$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x+2}}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537880 1072 #uuid "02054798-e30b-5bba-b50a-1ea23b8ac8ca" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537880 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x-1}{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5y-1}{k}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5y-1}{k}) \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡((5y-1)/k)} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{5y-1}{k} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ 5y-1 & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k \\\\ 5y & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k={3}^{2}=9.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537814 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537881 1069 "q-96306" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537881 1071 "Calculate $\\int ({cosh}^{3}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537881 1072 #uuid "032608f7-6edc-5183-bb2d-4ede3e9c1645" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537881 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({cosh}^{3}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x({cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537859 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537882 1069 "q-250505" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537882 1071 "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537882 1072 #uuid "04376890-0a44-56a8-b428-8110db81b255" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537882 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 4x-4 & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4x-4 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(y/4-1/2)} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & ={3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4})+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=4({3}^{4x-4})+2.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537774 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537883 1069 "q-79392" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537883 1071 "Find the exact solution to $3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4)-1=8.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537883 1072 #uuid "062c010f-c635-5129-83e7-3b14e937de12" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537883 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4)-1 & =8 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4) & =9 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x-4)}=2x-4.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & ={e}^{3} \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{3}+4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}+4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537832 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537884 1069 "q-101958" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537884 1071 "Given that $f(x)=-7x$, what is ${f}^{′}(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537884 1072 #uuid "0d6da41c-2674-5be8-ab4e-fb7795b804a5" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537884 1074 "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=-7x$,  we have that $f(x+h)=-7(x+h).$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-7(x+h)-(-7x)}{h}).\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537790 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537885 1069 "q-97017" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537885 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{cosh}^{2}⁡2x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537885 1072 #uuid "1212fe46-96e8-51be-81fa-22f7749ff713" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537885 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta ={cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x={cosh}^{2}⁡2x+{sinh}^{2}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{cosh}^{2}⁡2x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cosh}^{2}⁡2x+{sinh}^{2}⁡4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537764 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537886 1069 "q-97085" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537886 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-16{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537886 1072 #uuid "12bc09cc-29a3-5e9b-995b-e577844956a7" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537886 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-16{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[1-16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(9-8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(9x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =[9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)]-[9\\cdot 0-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0] \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2({2}^{2}-{2}^{-2}) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537772 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537844 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537863 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537887 1069 "q-351902" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537887 1071 "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({cosh}^{2}⁡x+3)\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n {{field-2}}\n$)\\,+\\,$\n {{field-3}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537887 1072 #uuid "12efb8aa-cbcc-5328-91fe-5d769b12ac7b" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537887 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({cosh}^{2}⁡x+3)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+\\frac{7}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\frac{7}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}]-[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537817 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537888 1069 "q-124720" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537888 1071 "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537888 1072 #uuid "1327ffff-07aa-5bea-93ff-453e6c9d98a5" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537888 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/4a/4a6bb0dd4991fe1a00bfedc7814f27c4a36b4114fa027673ed9cd03856fcc0d5.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537763 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537889 1069 "q-250510" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537889 1071 "If the function $f(x)=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{3})-k?$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+6)/3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537889 1072 #uuid "199584af-715c-57d8-b77d-cfea8258f652" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537889 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{3})-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3})-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3})-k \\\\ 3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3}) & =x+k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3}) & =\\frac{x+k}{3} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/3)} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y-1 & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=6.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537830 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537890 1069 "q-96466" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537890 1071 "Calculate $\\int (2{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{x}^{3}-2{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537890 1072 #uuid "1c959d7c-d68c-591a-a3b7-ea71063de12e" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537890 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4{x}^{3}-2{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2[{cosh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{sinh}^{2}⁡(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})]+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\cdot \\text{d}x+\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x+{x}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537840 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537891 1069 "q-97073" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537891 1071 "$\\int (4{sinh}^{2}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537891 1072 #uuid "1ddb9340-9af2-51a6-8581-bb4c21f42fab" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537891 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x$, we have\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡2x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{sinh}^{2}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x-1)+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537806 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537850 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537865 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537892 1069 "q-351898" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537892 1071 "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})+1)\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537892 1072 #uuid "1ebb4c16-c258-518a-a825-ce276a761a9f" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537892 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{3x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+1)+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537816 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537893 1069 "q-40946" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537893 1071 "![](images/e1/e1892e632e2485b17400ba9d2a9372bf164e20812c3b6fe4f9b03ab1348fab7d.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=f(x) \\\\ y=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537893 1072 #uuid "1ecf42ce-06ef-5bfd-b762-67d3f1697496" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537893 1074 "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/8d/8dfab4aa640e596227bd6a6a09af36264f6bce9bc0906a69ea433d6e6a3db78d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect at \n\n$\\,$ $(-1,1)\\,$ and $\\,(2,1).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537768 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537894 1069 "q-270354" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537894 1071 "Solve for $b$ where $\\frac{b+5}{b}+\\frac{b-7}{b}=6.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537894 1072 #uuid "21325d3f-ea32-5619-afd8-5e491ef497e5" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537894 1074 "First, we note that $b=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $b,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b+5}{b}+\\frac{b-7}{b} & =6 \\\\ b\\cdot (\\frac{b+5}{b}+\\frac{b-7}{b}) & =b\\cdot 6 \\\\ b\\cdot \\frac{b+5}{b}+b\\cdot \\frac{b-7}{b} & =b\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\cancel{b}\\cdot \\frac{b+5}{\\cancel{b}}+\\cancel{b}\\cdot \\frac{b-7}{\\cancel{b}} & =b\\cdot 6 \\\\ b+5+b-7 & =6b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $b,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b+5+b-7 & =6b \\\\ 2b-2 & =6b \\\\ -4b & =2 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $b=0$ as a solution. However, since $b=-\\frac{1}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $b=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537841 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537895 1069 "q-97010" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537895 1071 "$\\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+1)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-1))\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537895 1072 #uuid "216e3ef8-6751-5e3e-9d7f-5d48b7476c3e" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537895 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x={cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+1)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=\\int (2{cosh}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=\\int (2({cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C \\\\  & \\,=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537810 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537896 1069 "q-148367" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537896 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537896 1072 #uuid "2340627e-913d-5c02-af0a-4af27f0e0758" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537896 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k} \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2}{3}-x \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2-3x}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2y-k}) & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}) \\\\ 2y-k & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+\\frac{k}{2}$  \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=0.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537795 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537897 1069 "q-66777" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537897 1071 "What are the solutions of the equation $(x-3)^{2}=-9?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537897 1072 #uuid "239de5f3-ea6f-59ef-b333-b7c1243259a4" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537897 1074 "The left-hand side is a perfect square. Therefore, we can solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-9} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 3\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=3+3\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=3-3\\,\\text{i}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537781 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537898 1069 "q-97002" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537898 1071 "$\\int 4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537898 1072 #uuid "2aefbec7-dbc4-5c5a-8816-9b24bf877b40" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537898 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the double-angle formula for the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cosh}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x\\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537801 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537899 1069 "q-119056" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537899 1071 "A company spends $C(x)=0.01{x}^{2}+4x+680$ dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product. The company sells the product for $$12$ per unit. Given that the company can produce at most $500$ units per month, how many units should the company produce per month to maximize its monthly profit?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537899 1072 #uuid "3238715a-ab0d-5fbe-809b-2aa2ec76dbe8" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537899 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the company has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=12\\cdot x=12x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =12x-(0.01{x}^{2}+4x+680) \\\\  & =8x-0.01{x}^{2}-680.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 500.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<500.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=8-0.02x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 8-0.02x & =0 \\\\ x & =400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=400$ lies in the interval $0<x<500.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=400$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.02<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=400$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=400$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the company will have a maximum monthly profit when it produces $400$ units per month." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537851 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537900 1069 "q-165872" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537900 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537900 1072 #uuid "347de47e-5245-5fd2-b46a-2d80ee9ca643" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537900 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x(1+{sinh}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537759 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537901 1069 "q-148349" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537901 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+1}-2.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537901 1072 #uuid "34bef420-975f-5d0f-9f81-67d4bd446432" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537901 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+1}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1}-2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1} & =x+2 \\\\ {e}^{y+1} & =2x+4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537802 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537902 1069 "q-96435" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537902 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-1}{1+{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537902 1072 #uuid "36b4a778-aa5a-54d9-9b61-eb9431bef188" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537902 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cosecant-cotangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic cosecant-cotangent identity\n\n$$\n{coth}^{2}⁡x-1={csch}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-1}{1+{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{({coth}^{2}⁡x-1)({coth}^{2}⁡x+1)}{1+{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({coth}^{2}⁡x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)+\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-1}+\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{17}{15}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537793 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537903 1069 "q-126519" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537903 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{3}\\,dx-{x}^{3}\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537903 1072 #uuid "37e413b5-1ec6-5915-aa78-dfcf790b6b22" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537903 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{3}\\,dx-{x}^{3}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-3{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537821 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537904 1069 "q-86769" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537904 1071 "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537904 1072 #uuid "3a6241f6-827e-5f45-a2f2-3047ca1b82e0" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537904 1074 "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}18 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-48 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{38}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 18\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-48 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537761 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537905 1069 "q-250507" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537905 1071 "If the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{5})-k$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x-3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537905 1072 #uuid "3b5ffb6f-7067-559d-aec0-81e9ef0a06b7" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537905 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{5})-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5})-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5})-k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5}) & =x+k \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/5)} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{5} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ y-1 & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k} \\\\ y & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-3.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537833 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537906 1069 "q-109131" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537906 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,1)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+(xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537906 1072 #uuid "40abd682-d4fa-55d0-8dea-db00bcc211a6" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537906 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/56/5673a3eadfa3c01dbb9e2c8bf497b17f34a834d950621afa7d480af625ce1e18.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+(xy+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=y.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537827 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537907 1069 "q-64674" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537907 1071 "The angle $\\theta ={1032}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537907 1072 #uuid "44b12f59-5bfc-5de8-98fe-816ee27220b4" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537907 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with ${1032}^{∘}$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- ${1032}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}={672}^{∘}$\n\n- ${1032}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={312}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${312}^{∘}$ is coterminal with ${1032}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/6d/6db0d45744872518e73e249bc793e48792202c100bf42739fc889febb0fd6c32.png)\n\nWe see that ${312}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, ${1032}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537828 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537908 1069 "q-162603" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537908 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=4,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y\\sqrt{x}\\,i+(z-x)\\,j+{y}^{3}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537908 1072 #uuid "46a23183-289b-59ee-a9e5-bbe0aa79406a" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537908 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\sqrt{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\sqrt{x}]_{x=1}^{x=4}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537825 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537842 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537866 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537909 1069 "q-352792" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537909 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-4)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)-\\,$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537909 1072 #uuid "4b85bc81-6104-5328-b444-83c8973655d4" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537909 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-4)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1)-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(1)-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537862 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537910 1069 "q-551" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537910 1071 "${\\text{i}}^{24}=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537910 1072 #uuid "4c9f6489-bb99-5069-afdd-6f597a73df64" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537910 1074 "To find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=24.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n24÷4=6\\,\\text{R}0.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{24}={\\text{i}}^{0}=1.\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537855 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537911 1069 "q-87593" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537911 1071 "A factory spends $C(x)=100-47x+21{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ thousand dollars per month to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $$100\\,000$ per unit. Given that the factory can produce between $1$ and $10$ items per month, what is the maximum monthly profit it can make?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537911 1072 #uuid "4cb999cb-e9bf-5cd0-a21c-a3857e783014" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537911 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the factory has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=100\\cdot x=100x$ thousand dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =100x-(100-47x+21{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}+147x-100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 10.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-42x+147,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-42x+147 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-14x+49 & =0 \\\\ (x-7)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check if $x=7$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=6x-42.\n$$\n\nEvaluating ${P}^{″}(x)$ at the critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(7)=6(7)-42=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm or deny that $x=7$ is a maximum of $P(x).$ Therefore, we should consider $x=7$ as one of the candidates.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=7$ and $x=10.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =(1)^{3}-21(1)^{2}+147(1)-100=27 \\\\ P(7) & =(7)^{3}-21(7)^{2}+147(7)-100=243 \\\\ P(10) & =(10)^{3}-21(10)^{2}+147(10)-100=270\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is $270\\cdot $1\\,000=$270\\,000.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537826 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537912 1069 "q-96413" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537912 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537912 1072 #uuid "4e26e540-b6e1-55b9-88da-a99de2917513" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537912 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{sech}^{2}⁡x={tanh}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)+\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1}+\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{3}^{2}+1}{{3}^{2}-1}+\\frac{{2}^{2}+1}{{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537813 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537913 1069 "q-162562" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537913 1071 "![](images/cf/cf90197ac99a066db75154200de4cbee2494fef15deed6be7b7c4cd501ae8f26.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537913 1072 #uuid "50078542-09f5-53cd-a63a-27e043674d5d" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537913 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨4x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/dc/dc37a067eb8070ecbdfea53c1ede3e221e1873a192b50fcbf07234f33c183787.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=3$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨4x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =12{y}^{2}{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}12{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}12{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4{y}^{3}{z}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[8{z}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨4x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =8+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537757 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537914 1069 "q-88456" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537914 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x+2}-32\\cdot {5}^{x+2}=0?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537914 1072 #uuid "543d7533-dccc-5fe7-9798-fd9513ac4953" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537914 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x+2}-32\\cdot {5}^{x+2} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-32)\\cdot {5}^{x+2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-32=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x+2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-32=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-16) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+4)(x-4) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-4,4.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x+2}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x+2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-4,4,$ and their product is $(-4)\\cdot 4=-16.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537798 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537915 1069 "q-165894" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537915 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537915 1072 #uuid "56c765b2-a04b-54c3-b2ad-79e20147ade9" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537915 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x)-{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x) & =1 \\\\ 1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x) & ={cosh}^{2}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2(1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x))}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537803 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537916 1069 "q-162598" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537916 1071 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=\\pi ,$ $z=2,$ $z=4.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={z}^{3}\\,i+2xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537916 1072 #uuid "56f4d79a-05af-516b-82f5-f4f41e31d95b" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537916 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+0 \\\\  & =-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{2}^{4}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=2}^{z=4} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)\\cdot (\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537779 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537917 1069 "q-86685" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537917 1071 "$5{\\text{i}}^{-2}=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537917 1072 #uuid "5ff8f495-e68c-55fd-b502-1aeaa9ec649a" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537917 1074 "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n5{\\text{i}}^{-2}=\\frac{5}{{\\text{i}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe now recall that ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{{\\text{i}}^{2}}=\\frac{5}{(-1)}=-5.\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537848 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537918 1069 "q-148278" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537918 1071 "The function $f(x)={e}^{x+k}+3$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)+5.$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537918 1072 #uuid "6181c174-0cfd-5153-a1f3-884abcf7f2a6" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537918 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{x+k}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y+k}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y+k}+3 \\\\ {e}^{y+k} & =x-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3) \\\\ y+k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-k.$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-5.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537834 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537919 1069 "q-81645" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537919 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-k)$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-9}{3}.$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537919 1072 #uuid "6267302e-96c5-5885-be5f-0252c2b87de1" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537919 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-k) \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-k)} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ 3y-k & ={e}^{x} \\\\ 3y & ={e}^{x}+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}+k}{3}.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-9.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537856 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537920 1069 "q-162582" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537920 1071 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36$ and the vector field $F=(x+1)\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537920 1072 #uuid "65b5959b-427f-58b2-a1ab-3381594e4750" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537920 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z) \\\\  & =1+(-1)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $6$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}1\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =1{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {6}^{3} \\\\  & =288\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537878 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537921 1069 "q-250406" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537921 1071 "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{8}^{6x}}{3}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537921 1072 #uuid "675df8f0-06ab-563c-85f0-2a4a4ed296f3" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537921 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{8}^{6x}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3} \\\\ 3x & ={8}^{6y} \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(3x) & ={log}_{8}⁡({8}^{6y}) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(3x) & =6y \\\\ 6y & ={log}_{8}⁡(3x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537775 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537922 1069 "q-345609" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537922 1071 "An economist measured $4$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ for each currency in a collection under devaluation. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular currency.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=12,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=7,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=3,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe economist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $4\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537922 1072 #uuid "6923f3da-7346-5c88-a6fe-bd6d1873e88f" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537922 1074 "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(12)^{2}+(7)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =203\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the economist used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(12)^{2} \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{144}{203} \\\\  & ≈0.709 \\\\  & =70.9%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537860 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537867 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537923 1069 "q-351903" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537923 1071 "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-3{cosh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,-\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537923 1072 #uuid "6b367b19-8f35-5f68-a044-eb7b9764c38d" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537923 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-3{cosh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(4-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =[\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\sqrt{2})]-[0-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537794 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537924 1069 "q-73776" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537924 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{2}+5x+4?$\n\nI. $x=4$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537924 1072 #uuid "6b8e59d5-6b05-50ce-8455-191b0c2a0b7a" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537924 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x-4).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=4$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & ={4}^{2}+5(4)+4 \\\\  & =16+20+4 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=4$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x-4)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x-4)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537771 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537925 1069 "q-96307" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537925 1071 "Calculate $\\int (1-{sinh}^{2}⁡x{sech}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537925 1072 #uuid "6bf4783e-de85-5ad3-95bc-1eadaee336f8" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537925 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (1-{sinh}^{2}⁡x{sech}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-{sinh}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537847 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537926 1069 "q-96405" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537926 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537926 1072 #uuid "6c61796b-b24c-5403-8a9e-cd8bda63e7c0" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537926 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x={sech}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡(1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(1-\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537852 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537927 1069 "q-64828" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537927 1071 "The angle $\\theta =\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537927 1072 #uuid "6d153978-db48-529b-877e-2cfabb3e0039" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537927 1074 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ by *subtracting* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{4}$\n\n- $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/d0/d00b1363ef23bfd1e84ae968e1dc6ec4e36aa7f45d469c653b9f930cb0705cdf.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $\\frac{19\\pi }{4}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537864 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537928 1069 "q-362032" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537928 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,{coth}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537928 1072 #uuid "70e33acb-bebf-51b2-9607-6c52a3430a18" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537928 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡x-1={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n{coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,{coth}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{7}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x({coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x)}{{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{7}({coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{7}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{|}_{2}^{7} \\\\  & =7-2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537784 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537929 1069 "q-348024" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537929 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537929 1072 #uuid "7461b1f3-1aea-5d09-ab06-c9f25cb75e4f" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537929 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x={sech}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{tanh}^{2}⁡x}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot (1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x}{{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡0) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}+{e}^{-(0)}}{2}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537787 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537930 1069 "q-96453" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537930 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537930 1072 #uuid "7649fd25-c8c5-5938-a768-41b9d4f9aa06" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537930 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =2x-1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)-{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1) & =1 \\\\ {cosh}^{2}⁡(2x-1) & =1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{cosh}^{3}⁡(2x-1)}{{cosh}^{2}⁡(2x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537766 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537931 1069 "q-47689" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537931 1071 "Given that $f(x)=2x$, the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be computed using the definition of the derivative. The first few steps of this calculation are shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-2x}{h} \\\\  & =…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinish the calculation, and hence find ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537931 1072 #uuid "779ee099-0fc5-5dca-bd3b-ce3793ba0e8c" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537931 1074 "By proceeding with the remaining calculations we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-2x}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2x+2h-2x}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2h}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)=2.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537777 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537932 1069 "q-148328" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537932 1071 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{5},$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=\\frac{4}{5},$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\frac{3}{5}.$ Find $\\text{Var}[X-2Y].$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537932 1072 #uuid "7816847f-8647-5628-af20-0f9b03662f9a" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537932 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X-2Y] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}+(-2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{16}{5}-\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537838 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537933 1069 "q-226103" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537933 1071 "![](images/32/321c16127ae1999e4ec42024f35cd076930618588fe378b9ca16a4ee4eabd58a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-0.5x\\,i-0.5y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537933 1072 #uuid "7933960a-e5cd-5862-9088-b9c1f61b494c" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537933 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/abe90a592e098a4656577f3a2303669dccc126baff738397836c8f96c26b5389.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-0.5x\\,i-0.5y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-0.5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-4,4].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-0.5{\\int }_{-4}^{4}4\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2(4-(-4)) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-16)=-64.\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537822 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537934 1069 "q-165874" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537934 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2{sinh}^{2}⁡3x+2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537934 1072 #uuid "7b5373c3-faa7-5442-9a9c-2d5aebc96c49" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537934 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta ={cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x={cosh}^{2}⁡3x+{sinh}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{sinh}^{2}⁡3x+2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2({cosh}^{2}⁡3x+{sinh}^{2}⁡3x)-1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x-x){|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537868 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537935 1069 "q-73777" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537935 1071 "For which of the following polynomials is $(x+1)$ a factor?\n\nI. $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x+2$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}+8x+7$\n\nIII. $h(x)=-{x}^{2}+x$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537935 1072 #uuid "7c34a96f-d461-5b11-9cb5-80d7557e52d7" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537935 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+1).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, $(x+1)$ is a factor of $p(x)$ if the root $x=-1$ is a root of $p(x).$ \n\nSo now, we will check if $x=-1$ is a root of the polynomials $f,$ $g$ and $h.$\n\n- Evaluating $x=-1$ at $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+3(-1)+2 \\\\  & =1-3+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+1)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Now, evaluating $x=-1$ at $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+8(-1)+7 \\\\  & =1-8+7 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, by the factor theorem, we conclude that $(x+1)$ is indeed a factor of $g(x).$\n\n- Finally, evaluating $x=-1$ at $h(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-1) & =-(-1)^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537849 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537936 1069 "q-347278" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537936 1071 "A tutoring center offers private lesson packages. When the price of a package is $$40,$ the center books all $80$ available sessions. For every $$4$ increase in the package price, one fewer session is booked. If the cost of providing each session is $$16,$ what price should the center charge to maximize its profit?\n\nTo maximize profit, the center should charge $$$\n{{field-1}}\nper session." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537936 1072 #uuid "7f361b6d-88da-56f4-bb4f-1191d0218d94" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537936 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nLet $x$ represent the number of unbooked sessions. Then the number of **booked** sessions is $(80-x),$ and the **price per session** is $(40+4x).$\n\nSo, the total revenue is\n\n$$\nR(x)=(80-x)(40+4x).\n$$\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\nR(x)=-4{x}^{2}+280x+3200.\n$$\n\nThe cost of providing each booked session is $$16,$ so the total cost is\n\n$$\nC(x)=16(80-x)=1280-16x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =(-4{x}^{2}+280x+3200)-(1280-16x) \\\\  & =-4{x}^{2}+296x+1920.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given the domain $0\\le x\\le 80.$\n\nTo find the maximum, we take the derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-8x+296.\n$$\n\nSolving ${P}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get:\n\n$$\nx=37.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(x)=-8<0,$ this point is a maximum. Since $P(x)$ is quadratic, this maximum is global.\n\nNow, if $x=37$ sessions are unbooked, then the price is:\n\n$$\n40+4(37)=188.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize profit, the center should charge $$\\,\\,188$ per session." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537843 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537937 1069 "q-112375" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537937 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-64?$\n\nI. $x=-4$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x+4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537937 1072 #uuid "80bbf2bd-d1de-5b2e-a143-1c03752fb710" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537937 1074 "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+4).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-4$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =(-4)^{3}-64 \\\\  & =-64-64 \\\\  & =-128 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-4$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x+4)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x+4)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537854 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537938 1069 "q-75921" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537938 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x}=\\frac{5x-6}{2x}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537938 1072 #uuid "86933fa6-f8f7-5354-b325-40dd23c73f55" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537938 1074 "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $2x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x} & =\\frac{5x-6}{2x} \\\\ 2x\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x}) & =n\\cdot (\\frac{5x-6}{2x}) \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{2x}+2x\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{2x} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{5x-6}{2x} \\\\ \\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{2x}}+\\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{\\cancel{2x}} & =\\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{5x-6}{\\cancel{2x}} \\\\ x-2+x-4 & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2+x-4 & =5x-6 \\\\ 2x-6 & =5x-6 \\\\ 5x-2x & =-6+6 \\\\ 3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Therefore, we conclude that there is no solution." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537815 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537939 1069 "q-127193" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537939 1071 "![](images/32/32d4722f4a1dd1cfae3fb22cde7399dcb66623e484bcd5989fd2de246a905b51.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-3\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537939 1072 #uuid "90bd3eab-412f-52dc-83f5-2f8877931db1" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537939 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=-3\\,j$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net outflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537770 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537940 1069 "q-46621" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537940 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}).$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537940 1072 #uuid "9b1ff915-f7df-590e-9573-48667059c317" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537940 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\ {10}^{x} & ={10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(y/3)} \\\\ {10}^{x} & =\\frac{y}{3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {10}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {10}^{x}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537829 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537941 1069 "q-70040" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537941 1071 "If $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=20$, then what is the value of  $x?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537941 1072 #uuid "9bdc2bf5-cb58-518d-9b62-85ffcb2b1edc" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537941 1074 "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =20 \\\\ (2+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =20 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =20 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{20}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =4 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, $x={e}^{4}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537811 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537942 1069 "q-250479" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537942 1071 "If the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+x/2}+1$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1-x)-2,\n$$\n\n \nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537942 1072 #uuid "9d666229-2a75-553e-ab1a-3470df672efe" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537942 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+x/2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+y/2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+y/2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+y/2} & =x-1 \\\\ {10}^{1+y/2} & =kx-k \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1+y/2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k) \\\\ 1+\\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k) \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k)-1 \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=2\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k)-2.$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-1.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537804 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537943 1069 "q-347992" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537943 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537943 1072 #uuid "9ddcb9f3-50a1-505b-ae57-c8a55dc1f652" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537943 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{sech}^{2}⁡x={tanh}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{1-{sech}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x}{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{sech}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2-{2}^{-1}}-\\frac{4+{4}^{-1}}{4-{4}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{17}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537857 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537944 1069 "q-348034" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537944 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+{csch}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537944 1072 #uuid "9ea30f92-f367-5355-89e8-50b8c17d0449" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537944 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡x-1={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n1+{csch}^{2}⁡x={coth}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+{csch}^{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x(1+{csch}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{csch}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-{2}^{-1}}-\\frac{2}{3-{3}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537877 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537945 1069 "q-124736" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537945 1071 "Which angle is coterminal with $-{202}^{∘}?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537945 1072 #uuid "a22714ba-933f-5202-894c-587dd0012788" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537945 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in degrees), we add and subtract integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ to $-{202}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{202}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={158}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\n- $-{202}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={518}^{∘}$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than ${518}^{∘},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ from $-{202}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{202}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{562}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{202}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘}=-{922}^{∘}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-{922}^{∘},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is ${158}^{∘}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537818 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537946 1069 "q-148313" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537946 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={7}^{x-2}+4.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537946 1072 #uuid "a2f709a7-c2aa-55fd-a6a5-4424cd8b1fab" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537946 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={7}^{x-2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4 \\\\ x-4 & ={7}^{y-2} \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-4) & ={log}_{7}⁡({7}^{y-2}) \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-4) & =y-2 \\\\ y & ={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537839 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537947 1069 "q-340405" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537947 1071 "Use the definition of the derivative to find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if  $f(x)=-4{x}^{2}+5x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537947 1072 #uuid "a35127e9-e5f3-508e-9d5a-b3279aa78b1a" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537947 1074 "The definition of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=-4{x}^{2}+5x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-4(x+h)^{2}+5(x+h)-(-4{x}^{2}+5x)}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-4({x}^{2}+2xh+{h}^{2})+5x+5h+4{x}^{2}-5x}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-4{x}^{2}-8xh-4{h}^{2}+5x+5h+4{x}^{2}-5x}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-8xh-4{h}^{2}+5h}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{h(-8x-4h+5)}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(-8x-4h+5) \\\\  & =-8x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537948 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537807 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537948 1069 "q-1679" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537948 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537948 1071 "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =-{390}^{∘}?$\n\nI. $-{30}^{∘}$\n\nII. ${305}^{∘}$\n\nIII. $-{750}^{∘}$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537948 1072 #uuid "a369ef6f-b58e-5e3b-af34-1de431b7ed56" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537948 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}:$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ to $-{390}^{∘}$:\n\n- $-{390}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\n- $-{390}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={330}^{∘}$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than ${330}^{∘}.$ So we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ from $-{390}^{∘}$\n\n- $-{390}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{750}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, only $-{30}^{∘}$ and $-{750}^{∘}$ are coterminal with $-{390}^{∘}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I and III only." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537949 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537789 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537949 1069 "q-96429" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537949 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537949 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{2-2{tanh}^{4}⁡x}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537949 1072 #uuid "a49f44d3-1abb-5067-966c-70ae50d2155e" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537949 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x={sech}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{2-2{tanh}^{4}⁡x}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1-{tanh}^{4}⁡x}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x)}{1+{tanh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+1}-2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{0}-1}{{e}^{0}+1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{2}-1}{{2}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537765 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537950 1069 "q-3518" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537950 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{3-x}{3x}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537950 1072 #uuid "a581df95-2d51-59ad-a71c-d2138e307e87" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537950 1074 "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nThe equation has the denominators $x$ and $3x.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,3x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3x,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{x}) & =3x\\cdot (\\frac{3-x}{3x}) \\\\ 3x\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} & =3x\\cdot \\frac{3-x}{3x} \\\\ \\cancel{3}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}+3\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{x}} & =\\cancel{3x}\\cdot \\frac{3-x}{\\cancel{3x}} \\\\ x\\cdot (2)+3\\cdot (1) & =3-x \\\\ 2x+3 & =3-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =3-x \\\\ 2x+x & =3-3 \\\\ 3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=0,$ we conclude that there is no solution." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537872 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537951 1069 "q-345594" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537951 1071 "An epidemiologist measured $4$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},$ for each disease affecting a specific population. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular individual.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=11,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=7,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe epidemiologist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $4\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537951 1072 #uuid "a62d30b2-87c9-5394-8f0b-7a8e71495548" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537951 1074 "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(11)^{2}+(7)^{2}+(5)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =199\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the epidemiologist used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(11)^{2} \\\\  & =121.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{121}{199} \\\\  & ≈0.608 \\\\  & =60.8%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537861 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537952 1069 "q-22983" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537952 1071 "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}?$\n\nI. $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$\n\nII. $\\frac{14\\pi }{3}$\n\nIII. $-\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537952 1072 #uuid "a6cfd9d2-1fde-532f-8824-00cee2a76d89" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537952 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{14\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $\\frac{14\\pi }{3}.$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$\n\n- $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, all three are coterminal with $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I, II, and III." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537874 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537953 1069 "q-101957" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537953 1071 "Given that $f(x)=2x+1$, what is ${f}^{′}(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537953 1072 #uuid "ab9ad28a-3195-5102-ad22-90c2dc511297" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537953 1074 "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=2x+1$,  we have that $f(x+h)=2(x+h)+1.$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2(x+h)+1-(2x+1)}{h}).\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537762 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537954 1069 "q-13587" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537954 1071 "If $f(x)=-3x-5$ and $g(x)=-5x+12,$ then $(f-g)(x)=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537954 1072 #uuid "ae7dac11-f018-50fc-a556-53f9ec9e3fc0" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537954 1074 "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f-g)(x) & =f(x)-g(x) \\\\  & =(-3x-5)-(-5x+12) \\\\  & =(-3x-(-5x))-(5+12) \\\\  & =2x-17\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537858 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537955 1069 "q-126546" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537955 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},\\,x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537955 1072 #uuid "af0d631e-6f1f-57fd-bd84-ccd6cde384ee" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537955 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0c/0cb340ef33d4cc38081e3796b4c8e076f1499760c0a3ffeecb8123a41fefeec8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537797 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537956 1069 "q-124757" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537956 1071 "Which angle is coterminal with $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537956 1072 #uuid "af0faf42-b43a-5224-af78-09641aa5c63d" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537956 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $ (in radians), we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$:\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}+2\\pi =\\frac{19\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{29\\pi }{5}\\,✓$\n\nThere are no answer choices larger than $\\frac{29\\pi }{5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$:\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{11\\pi }{5}$\n\n- $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}-3\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{21\\pi }{5}$\n\nThere are no answer choices smaller than $-\\frac{21\\pi }{5},$ so we can stop here.\n\nSo the correct answer is $\\frac{29\\pi }{5}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537786 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537957 1069 "q-114073" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537957 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537957 1072 #uuid "affed96b-3c3d-54a9-a279-f8432cb1a012" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537957 1074 "Note that we can write this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{2}{5+20{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537824 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537958 1069 "q-100751" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537958 1071 "If $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=9-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$, then what is the value of  $x?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537958 1072 #uuid "b08ed48d-cd63-5608-bd95-a1073f0e3d2b" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537958 1074 "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =9-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =9 \\\\ (1+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =9 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =9 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537791 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537959 1069 "q-66431" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537959 1071 "The equation ${x}^{2}-10x+50=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537959 1072 #uuid "b2554883-e4a0-51fb-ab55-70bbe918132c" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537959 1074 "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+50 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x & =-50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x & =-50 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =-50 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}-25 & =-50 \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5,\\,q=-25,$ and $p+q=-30.$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-25} \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 5\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =5\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=5+5\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=5-5\\,\\text{i}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537805 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537960 1069 "q-250404" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537960 1071 "The inverse of the function $f(x)={e}^{2-x}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537960 1072 #uuid "b31f5c4b-dccc-5d6d-b26c-76fe9889b868" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537960 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2-y} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-y}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =2-y \\\\ y & =2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537845 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537961 1069 "q-569" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537961 1071 "If $a={\\text{i}}^{2}$ and $b={\\text{i}}^{9}$, then $ab=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537961 1072 #uuid "b7f8ea24-bf7b-5ebd-ac96-307434b99a43" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537961 1074 "Using the addition law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\nab={\\text{i}}^{2}\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{9}={\\text{i}}^{2+9}={\\text{i}}^{11}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\text{i}$ raised to some positive integer power $n$, we divide $n$ by $4,$ and raise $\\text{i}$ to the remainder.\n\nIn our case, we have $n=11.$ Dividing the power by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n11÷4=2\\,\\text{R}3.\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{11}={\\text{i}}^{3}=-\\text{i}.\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537773 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537962 1069 "q-88461" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537962 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4\\cdot {5}^{x-2}={x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-2}?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537962 1072 #uuid "bb3bd545-e795-5869-ab36-2a8ac7b0ac72" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537962 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {5}^{x-2} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-2} \\\\ 4\\cdot {5}^{x-2}-{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-2} & =0 \\\\ (4-{x}^{2}){5}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4-{x}^{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4-{x}^{2}=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2+x)(2-x)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-2,2.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x-2}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x-2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-2,2,$ and their product is $(-2)\\cdot 2=-4.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537835 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537963 1069 "q-352419" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537963 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537963 1072 #uuid "bc295d1d-76d5-5f67-a844-a3b5be954577" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537963 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n2{sinh}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(6+6{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(2+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x+x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{2}^{2}-{2}^{-2}}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{45}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537837 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537964 1069 "q-46622" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537964 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537964 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(x)+8.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537964 1072 #uuid "bc418213-398e-54af-8583-a0bcd69d268b" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537964 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equations, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)+8 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(y) & =x-8 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(y)} & ={2}^{x-8} \\\\ y & ={2}^{x-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x-8}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537853 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537965 1069 "q-46629" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537965 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x).$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537965 1072 #uuid "bf8490ce-55f6-59c1-8771-a458611eb22b" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537965 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7y) \\\\ {e}^{x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7y)} \\\\ {e}^{x} & =7y \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{7}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537869 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537966 1069 "q-19176" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537966 1071 "Find the exact solution to $5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)=15.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537966 1072 #uuid "c0394c3f-264a-5afa-ba91-982051eae5c0" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537966 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & =15 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}=2x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={e}^{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537796 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537967 1069 "q-165895" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537967 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{6}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537967 1072 #uuid "c7a3ee0b-1bd5-5d07-b45d-54312bc57611" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537967 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =3x+2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)-{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2) & =1 \\\\ 1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2) & ={cosh}^{2}⁡(3x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{6}{{cosh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡(3x+2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {sech}^{2}⁡(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x+2)+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x+2)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537820 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537968 1069 "q-86762" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537968 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537968 1072 #uuid "cc66ebbe-2cca-5407-87bc-772df677089f" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537968 1074 "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}17 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537812 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537969 1069 "q-345553" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537969 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the third principal component of the data?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537969 1072 #uuid "cd69e9f7-e929-5fe2-b3bb-4bb36c948590" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537969 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the third principal component is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537776 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537970 1069 "q-165889" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537970 1071 "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537970 1072 #uuid "cf4092b0-8e64-55b4-94ba-2a8ea4d2ac81" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537970 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡3x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2{cosh}^{2}⁡3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(3-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(2-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(2x-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡6x)|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =[2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})]-[2\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537846 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537971 1069 "q-352425" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537971 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537971 1072 #uuid "d0857a55-78b2-5e38-b682-a21c51974c3c" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537971 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[2-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(8-6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(8x-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =[8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)]-[0-0] \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3(\\frac{{3}^{2}-{3}^{-2}}{2}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{40}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537800 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537972 1069 "q-162576" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537972 1071 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z+3)^{2}=16$ and the vector field $F=(x+2y)\\,i+(4z-y)\\,j+(z+xy)\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537972 1072 #uuid "d1625297-e3fd-57e3-914d-8b51c40eca40" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537972 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4z-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z+xy) \\\\  & =1+(-1)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $4$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}1\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =1{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =\\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {4}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{256\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537758 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537973 1069 "q-97004" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537973 1071 "$\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x(2-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537973 1072 #uuid "d2739611-7ef4-5493-8191-67c702a22c88" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537973 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the double-angle formula for the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cosh}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =7x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡14x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x\\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡14x=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x(2-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡14x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡7x\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡14x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{28}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡7x-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡14x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537870 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537974 1069 "q-148339" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537974 1071 "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=3,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=4,$ and second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=8$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=15.$ Find the variance of $5X+2Y.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537974 1072 #uuid "d31ef211-c21b-5026-971f-34b7088e4f67" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537974 1074 "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =8-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =15-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =4-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[5X+2Y] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot 4+{2}^{2}\\cdot 6+2\\cdot 5\\cdot 2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =100+24-40 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537876 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537975 1069 "q-348041" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537975 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1+{csch}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537975 1072 #uuid "d50512b3-2d0b-53b0-a169-ed8ad3f44908" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537975 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡x-1={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n1+{csch}^{2}⁡x={coth}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1+{csch}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡8)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-{8}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{2-{2}^{-1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{16}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537767 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537976 1069 "q-250405" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537976 1071 "The inverse of the function $f(x)=-{3}^{4x}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537976 1072 #uuid "d6cea5e8-bf96-5756-ad7c-875f953f91a2" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537976 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{3}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y} \\\\ -x & ={3}^{4y} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(-x) & ={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{4y}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(-x) & =4y \\\\ 4y & ={log}_{3}⁡(-x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537780 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537977 1069 "q-87576" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537977 1071 "A daycare center can take care of between $10$ and $20$ children. The fee that parents must pay each month depends on the number of children enrolled in the center. If there are $10$ children enrolled, parents must pay $$40.$ On the other hand, if there are more than $10$ children enrolled, the price drops by $$2$ for each child. All the parents pay the same monthly fee.\n\nHow many children must enroll for the center to maximize its revenue?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537977 1072 #uuid "da64bda4-339a-50c3-828b-c79ac0fe60b0" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537977 1074 "First, note that if there are $10+x$ children enrolled, then the monthly fee is $40-2x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(10+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of children enrolled}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{(40-2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{monthly fee}} \\\\  & =(10+x)(40-2x) \\\\  & =400-20x+40x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =400+20x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the center can only take care between $10$ and $20$ children, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & \\le x+10\\le 20 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=20-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 20-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =20 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=5$ lies in the interval $0<x<10.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=5$ does indeed give a maximum value of $R(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=5$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=5$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the center must have $10+5=15$ children enrolled to maximize its revenue." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537778 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537978 1069 "q-345562" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537978 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be an $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the second principal component of the data?" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537978 1072 #uuid "dcb3950f-e511-5b2b-8452-0fa93d40b2ff" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537978 1074 "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $m\\times m$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $m\\times m$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the second principal component is ${v}_{2}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537783 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537979 1069 "q-148359" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537979 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537979 1072 #uuid "e0086fad-533f-5e80-a92f-0ac75b17640c" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537979 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y} & =x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {3}^{2y} & =4x+2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{2y}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2).$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537875 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537980 1069 "q-78292" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537980 1071 "![](images/e6/e6831eff5d8feb87ebba8e7d449d98fbe72c3ed4df01d8374d4866e8e3456ddc.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+1 \\\\ y={x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537980 1072 #uuid "e75342f3-2efc-5d3d-a3c3-d44c19646c04" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537980 1074 "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/e1/e1ebbb0ce147dc986dd4d47539f055407c9b870af2ada9629520c4fa1d9aef33.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect at \n\n$\\,$ $(-2,3)\\,$ and $\\,(1,0).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537769 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537981 1069 "q-86688" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537981 1071 "${\\text{i}}^{-1}=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537981 1072 #uuid "e87479bf-28b6-5cab-9b25-26c4f45c1fed" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537981 1074 "First, using the laws of exponents, we can write\n\n$$\n{\\text{i}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\text{i}}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $\\text{i},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\text{i}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{i}}{\\text{i}}=\\frac{\\text{i}}{{\\text{i}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now recall that ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{i}}{{\\text{i}}^{2}}=\\frac{\\text{i}}{(-1)}=-\\text{i}.\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537785 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537982 1069 "q-165868" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537982 1071 "Calculate\n$\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x(1-4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537982 1072 #uuid "e9856d89-c407-5a27-9255-59fdcff13d87" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537982 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the double-angle formula for the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x(1-4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537799 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537983 1069 "q-110775" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537983 1071 "If $3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3=3,$ what is the value of $x?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537983 1072 #uuid "e999e0ab-2ba5-58ed-8052-a9586c979824" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537983 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+3 & =3 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nx={e}^{0}=1.\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537809 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537984 1069 "q-75936" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537984 1071 "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m}=\\frac{1}{9m}?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537984 1072 #uuid "f095dc29-06a2-59bf-8997-6e524951d317" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537984 1074 "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3m,6m,$ and $9m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3m,6m,9m)=18m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $18m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m} & =\\frac{1}{9m} \\\\ 18m\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m}) & =18m\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9m}) \\\\ 18m\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{3m})-18m\\cdot (\\frac{m-1}{6m}) & =18m\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9m}) \\\\ 6\\cdot 3m\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{3m})-3\\cdot 6m\\cdot (\\frac{m-1}{6m}) & =2\\cdot 9m\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9m}) \\\\ 6\\cdot \\cancel{3m}\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{\\cancel{3m}})-3\\cdot \\cancel{6m}\\cdot (\\frac{m-1}{\\cancel{6m}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{9m}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{9m}}) \\\\ 6(4m+1)-3(m-1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(4m+1)-3(m-1) & =2 \\\\ 24m+6-3m+3 & =2 \\\\ 21m+9 & =2 \\\\ 21m & =-7 \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=-\\frac{1}{3}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537819 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537985 1069 "q-96522" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537985 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{csch}^{3}⁡(7x-2)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537985 1072 #uuid "f321e82d-23c7-5f15-828b-dbb232e439d7" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537985 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cosecant-cotangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic cosecant-cotangent identity\n\n$$\n{coth}^{2}⁡\\theta -1={csch}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =7x-2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{coth}^{2}⁡(7x-2)-1 & ={csch}^{2}⁡(7x-2) \\\\ {coth}^{2}⁡(7x-2) & ={csch}^{2}⁡(7x-2)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{csch}^{3}⁡(7x-2)+\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)[{csch}^{2}⁡(7x-2)+1]}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2){coth}^{2}⁡(7x-2)}{\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)\\operatorname{coth}⁡(7x-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{csch}⁡(7x-2)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537823 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537986 1069 "q-250483" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537986 1071 "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537986 1072 #uuid "f4092e37-c259-56c3-8698-e5c1a205391f" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537986 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ 2x-1 & ={log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & ={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡y} \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & =y \\\\ y & ={2}^{2x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{2x-1}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537782 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537987 1069 "q-96997" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537987 1071 "Calculate\n$\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(2+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537987 1072 #uuid "f63f0268-1410-54a1-bbf2-06e90b1cc575" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537987 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the double-angle formula for the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\n$$\n\nRearranging the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x(2+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537871 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537988 1069 "q-75955" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537988 1071 "Which value of $n$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}=\\frac{7}{3n}?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537988 1072 #uuid "f7541a79-898f-52f6-9741-385d5ef8e2c1" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537988 1074 "We note that $n=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $2n,$ ${n}^{2},$ and $3n.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2n,{n}^{2},3n)=6{n}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6{n}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{7}{3n} \\\\ 6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}) & =6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{7}{3n}) \\\\ 6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2n})-6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}) & =6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{7}{3n}) \\\\ 3n\\cdot 2n\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2n})-6\\cdot {n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}) & =2n\\cdot 3n\\cdot (\\frac{7}{3n}) \\\\ 3n\\cdot \\cancel{2n}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{2n}})-6\\cdot \\cancel{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1-n}{\\cancel{{n}^{2}}}) & =2n\\cdot \\cancel{3n}\\cdot (\\frac{7}{\\cancel{3n}}) \\\\ 3n\\cdot (3)-6\\cdot (1-n) & =2n\\cdot (7) \\\\ 9n-6(1-n) & =14n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $n,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9n-6(1-n) & =14n \\\\ 15n-6 & =14n \\\\ n & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $n=0$ as a solution. But since $n=6\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $n=6.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537831 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537989 1069 "q-115795" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537989 1071 "Solve for $q$ where $\\frac{2q-1}{4q}=\\frac{q+1}{3q}-1.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537989 1072 #uuid "fa991cba-7607-5ad7-99b3-e6c6f2993b9f" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537989 1074 "We note that $q=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3q$ and $4q.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3q,4q)=12q.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12q,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2q-1}{4q} & =\\frac{q+1}{3q}-1 \\\\ 12q\\cdot (\\frac{2q-1}{4q}) & =12q\\cdot (\\frac{q+1}{3q}-1) \\\\ 12q\\cdot \\frac{2q-1}{4q} & =12q\\cdot \\frac{q+1}{3q}-12q\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\cancel{4q}\\cdot (\\frac{2q-1}{\\cancel{4q}}) & =4\\cdot \\cancel{3q}\\cdot (\\frac{q+1}{\\cancel{3q}})-12q \\\\ 3(2q-1) & =4(q+1)-12q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $q,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2q-1) & =4(q+1)-12q \\\\ 6q-3 & =4q+4-12q \\\\ 6q-3 & =-8q+4 \\\\ 6q+8q & =4+3 \\\\ 14q & =7 \\\\ q & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $q=0$ as a solution. But since $q=\\frac{1}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $q=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537792 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537990 1069 "q-148370" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537990 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537990 1072 #uuid "fcede9b9-3aa7-543c-ba0b-831f64ff8671" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537990 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky} & =x-2 \\\\ {3}^{ky} & =\\frac{5x-10}{2} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{ky}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ ky & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=1.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537760 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537991 1069 "q-348060" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537991 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x{coth}^{2}⁡x+x{csch}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537991 1072 #uuid "fe6b40d3-8971-55c8-aabc-4ac03f347ec4" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537991 1074 "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the cotangent-cosecant identity \n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic identity\n\n$$\n{coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x{coth}^{2}⁡x+x{csch}^{2}⁡x}{{coth}^{4}⁡x-{csch}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x({coth}^{2}⁡x+{csch}^{2}⁡x)}{({coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x)({coth}^{2}⁡x+{csch}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x}{({coth}^{2}⁡x-{csch}^{2}⁡x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537808 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537992 1069 "q-276869" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537992 1071 "Solve for $v$ where $\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v}=\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}}.$\n\n$v=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537992 1072 #uuid "ff75b330-ecd4-5fa0-b36d-564d6c66e864" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537992 1074 "We note that $v=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $6v,\\,{v}^{2},$ and $2{v}^{2}.$ First, we find the denominators' least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$). We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(6v,{v}^{2},2{v}^{2})=6{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6{v}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v} & =\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6{v}^{2}(\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v}) & =6{v}^{2}(\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}}) \\\\ 6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{6v} & =6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-6v\\cdot v\\cdot \\frac{7}{6v} & =3\\cdot 2{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6\\cdot \\cancel{{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{\\cancel{{v}^{2}}}-\\cancel{6v}\\cdot v\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\cancel{6v}} & =3\\cdot \\cancel{2{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{2{v}^{2}}} \\\\ 6(v+4)-v\\cdot (7) & =3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 6(v+4)-7v & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $v,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(v+4)-7v & =15 \\\\ 6v+24-7v & =15 \\\\ -v+24 & =15 \\\\ -v & =-9 \\\\ v & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $v=0$ as a solution. But since $v=9\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $v=9.$" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537993 1058 #edb/ref 17592186537997 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537993 1062 #uuid "00083c00-24dc-506e-a324-73b019d6c818" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537993 1063 #edb/ref 17592186537958 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537994 1058 #edb/ref 17592186537993 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537994 1062 #uuid "013f2eef-7094-5b40-95fe-1315ad5661d1" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537994 1063 #edb/ref 17592186537883 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537995 1058 #edb/ref 17592186538054 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537995 1062 #uuid "06674b92-79e6-5824-abba-71763f29a6cc" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537995 1063 #edb/ref 17592186537904 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537996 1058 #edb/ref 17592186538070 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537996 1062 #uuid "0e208ebe-b82a-51f2-b53a-db4dfe06f999" 13194139535892 17592186045417 true] [17592186537996 1063 #edb/ref 17592186497356 13194139535892 1759